A static HTML component produces markup. Its behaviour starts only when the
selected page's runtime code asks for it.

### Start with the initializer already in use

Moth's documentation sidebar marks the active link and builds its breadcrumb
through an explicit runtime call after the static fragments:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @styles sidebar, init_docs_sidebar

    [sidebar]

    init_docs_sidebar()!
]

The sidebar template runs nothing when it is imported or rendered.
`init_docs_sidebar()` calls the existing `@web/page` browser package, which
reads the current location and updates the navigation elements.

### Prefer native HTML and CSS

A checkbox and CSS can manage the sidebar's simple open and closed state without
Moth receiving an event. Reach for browser-native HTML and CSS whenever the
behaviour needs no application logic. A script that repeats a browser feature
adds state to keep in sync for no gain.

### The intended DOM boundary

The planned `@web/dom` package will provide typed operations for finding
elements, reading properties and geometry, setting attributes and updating the
live document. Ordinary Moth functions will own the page-specific algorithm:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    const component   -> static HTML and tracked CSS
    @entry runtime    -> explicit init function
    init function     -> @web/dom operations
    browser DOM       -> visible page update
]

For example, a future sidebar initializer can measure the navbar and set a CSS
custom property that controls the sidebar's top edge. That algorithm belongs to
the docs package. The generic DOM binding supplies primitives, such as a
measured rectangle.

This is a **future package boundary**, not a claim that `@web/dom` is available
today. The browser adapter will still use JavaScript internally. Application
code should not need to embed JavaScript source or script listeners in its
component templates.

### Later: event-driven updates

The intended `@web/events` package will connect browser events to typed Moth
messages and Channels. A sidebar could then react to scroll, resize and user
expansion without an authored JavaScript file. Until the Channel and
host-ingress runtime exists, that behaviour stays deferred.

See @../../packages/builder/page/ (@web/page) and @../../wiring (Wiring).
